* DONE [#A] <<14.1>> Mode-detection walk-up misclassifies a nested base install as "sub"
CLOSED: [2026-10-08 Thu] SCHEDULED: <2026-09-23 Wed>
:PROPERTIES:
:ARCHIVE_TIME: 2026-10-09 Fri 00:53
:ARCHIVE_FILE: /bxRepos/bisos-pip/aiActivity/AI-WorkPlan.org
:ARCHIVE_OLPATH: Stage 14 TODOs
:ARCHIVE_CATEGORY: AI-WorkPlan
:ARCHIVE_TODO: DONE
:END:
Found while testing Stage 12.1: =refresh= detects base-vs-sub by
walking up from the *parent* of the target directory looking for an
ancestor =AI-WORKFLOW.org= symlink under templatesBase. If the target
directory sits inside another already-=initiate=-d project (e.g. this
repo's own =py3/tests/=, self-hosted), the walk-up finds the *ancestor's*
symlink and calls it "sub" even when the target directory has its own
genuine base-mode =AI-WORKFLOW.org=. Affects any project nested inside
another initiated project, not just this repo's =tests/= dir.
----- AI Results: ------

Fixed by changing what decides the mode. =refresh= (and formerly =aiResume=)
walked up for any ancestor =AI-WORKFLOW.org= under the resolved base; now a
directory with its *own* =AI-WORKFLOW.org= symlink is a base install, and one
without it, below an initiated ancestor (of any templates base), is a sub
(=_findInitiatedAncestor=). =aiResume= itself was retired the same day.
- Verified (scratch): a base =initiate=-d inside an initiated project reports
  =MODE: base=; a homogeneous and a heterogeneous sub report =MODE: sub=.
- Also in this repo: =refresh= in =py3/tests/= reported =MODE: sub= and upgraded
  its legacy symlinked =CLAUDE.md= to the slim overlay copy (the quirk seen when
  testing there).

